   "subject": "colour gradient as in figtree",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Is it possible to mimic the behaviour of figtree where the colour of the branches is determined based on the status of the node they connect to and then a gradient applied E.g. <div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-VYfZnS_sFmI/WNAN6aSIUwI/AAAAAAAABUk/HaX-Hehi37EQaoMLCsuAm_Ux99hbpLimwCLcB/s1600/Screen%2BShot%2B2017-03-20%2Bat%2B17.14.02.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-VYfZnS_sFmI/WNAN6aSIUwI/AAAAAAAABUk/HaX-Hehi37EQaoMLCsuAm_Ux99hbpLimwCLcB/s1600/Screen%252BShot%252B2017-03-20%252Bat%252B17.14.02.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792482779689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3c-BMDqB9sjnP--iJ-jDJG\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-VYfZnS_sFmI/WNAN6aSIUwI/AAAAAAAABUk/HaX-Hehi37EQaoMLCsuAm_Ux99hbpLimwCLcB/s320/Screen%2BShot%2B2017-03-20%2Bat%2B17.14.02.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"292\" height=\"320\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Rather than this - </p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-a3bPB7VSMF4/WNAOPUP91bI/AAAAAAAABUo/aBvTqs5iAYgseJzd8Sq5Mg0W-7X94PWQQCLcB/s1600/Screen%2BShot%2B2017-03-20%2Bat%2B17.15.19.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-a3bPB7VSMF4/WNAOPUP91bI/AAAAAAAABUo/aBvTqs5iAYgseJzd8Sq5Mg0W-7X94PWQQCLcB/s1600/Screen%252BShot%252B2017-03-20%252Bat%252B17.15.19.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792482779689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1RvJAwBnu1eKPepNV6qA_2\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-a3bPB7VSMF4/WNAOPUP91bI/AAAAAAAABUo/aBvTqs5iAYgseJzd8Sq5Mg0W-7X94PWQQCLcB/s320/Screen%2BShot%2B2017-03-20%2Bat%2B17.15.19.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"313\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">In FigTree this is done with the Gradient check box under Appearance.</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">I can achieve the appearance without the gradient in ggtree, e.g. where location is a discrete trait and location.prob the posterior probability of that trait:</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">pal = brewer.pal(7,&quot;Set1&quot;)</p><p style=\"clear:both\">ggtree(beast_tree, mrsd=df$dt) +</p><p style=\"clear:both\">   geom_tree(aes(color=as.factor(location), size=location.prob)) + </p><p style=\"clear:both\">   theme_tree2() +</p><p style=\"clear:both\">   scale_color_manual(values = pal) +</p><p style=\"clear:both\">   scale_size(&quot;line&quot;, range=c(0, 1.2), guide=FALSE)<br></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">Is it possible to adapt this code to look like the former?</p><p style=\"clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">Thanks</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
