gleusercontent.com/-a3bPB7VSMF4/WNAOPUP91bI/AAAAAAAABUo/aBvTqs5iAYgseJzd8Sq5Mg0W-7X94PWQQCLcB/s320/Screen%2BShot%2B2017-03-20%2Bat%2B17.15.19.png\" width=\"320\" height=\"313\" border=\"0\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">In FigTree this is done with the Gradient check box under Appearance.</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">I can achieve the appearance without the gradient in ggtree, e.g. where location is a discrete trait and location.prob the posterior probability of that trait:</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">pal = brewer.pal(7,&quot;Set1&quot;)</p><p style=\"clear:both\">ggtree(beast_tree, mrsd=df$dt) +</p><p style=\"clear:both\">   geom_tree(aes(color=as.factor(location), size=location.prob)) + </p><p style=\"clear:both\">   theme_tree2() +</p><p style=\"clear:both\">   scale_color_manual(values = pal) +</p><p style=\"clear:both\">   scale_size(&quot;line&quot;, range=c(0, 1.2), guide=FALSE)<br></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">Is it possible to adapt this code to look like the former?</p><p style=\"clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">Thanks</p><span><font color=\"#888888\"><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
